   347	/// Judges whether packet loss might indicate a drop in MTU
   348	///
   349	/// Our MTU black hole detection scheme is a heuristic based on the order in which packets were sent
   350	/// (the packet number order), their sizes, and which are deemed lost.
   351	///
   352	/// First, contiguous groups of lost packets ("loss bursts") are aggregated, because a group of
   353	/// packets all lost together were probably lost for the same reason.
   354	///
   355	/// A loss burst is deemed "suspicious" if it contains no packets that are (a) smaller than the
   356	/// minimum MTU or (b) smaller than a more recent acknowledged packet, because such a burst could be
   357	/// fully explained by a reduction in MTU.
   358	///
   359	/// When the number of suspicious loss bursts exceeds [`BLACK_HOLE_THRESHOLD`], we judge the
   360	/// evidence for an MTU black hole to be sufficient.
